  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="cfb3-4c7a-54f7-b92e" name="Later Byzantine (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #79 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). As mentioned above, complacency following the successes of the 10th and early 11th centuries led to the running down of the armed forces and the failure of the Thematic system. The Empire therefore increasingly supplemented its’ professional troops with a wide variety of mercenaries, some of which formed into long standing regular units, and thus are not counted as Unreliable. The Empire limped on under increasing pressure until it suffered the ignominy of assault by the Fourth Crusade, which sacked Constantinople in 1204. The list may be used to create armies of the Macedonian (1041-1081) and Comnenian (1081-1185) dynasties. 

ARMY COMPOSITIONS (pick one in the Army composition list):
[Later Byzantine - Comnenian dynasty]
  Allies: up to 33%
  Cavalry: up to 75%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 50%
  Characters: Up to 25% Cavalry: Up to 75%. Every second unit may be guards. Infantry: Up to 50% Special Units: Up to 33% Allies: Up to 33%: Taken from the Steppe Nomads (Alans, Pechenegs or Cumans), Slav &amp; Bulgar (later Bulgar cavalry), First Crusade or Later Crusader lists. Artillery: It may also take Light Bolt Throwers (without cart) from the Late Roman list with one per 1,200 pts.
[Later Byzantine - Macedonian dynasty]
  Allies: up to 33%
  Cavalry: at least 25%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 75%
  Characters: Up to 25% Cavalry: At least 25% Infantry: Up to 75% Special Units: Up to 25% Allies: Up to 33%: Steppe Nomads list only (Alans, Pechenegs). Artillery: It may also take Light Bolt Throwers (without cart) from the Late Roman list with one per 1,200 pts.</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
